#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "f781ef9b-491f-5df4-a273-94f30ce79185" :question/math-academy-id "e-389" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)=(2x-4)^2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a left Riemann sum with $4$ rectangles of equal width." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/56/56427bec8108b822c4e1980725073cedc9fc79575d606014f72ea6ca9f8959c2.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A≈(16+9+4+1)\\cdot 0.5=15\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068554 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "6220f2e1-e654-5a30-a0f8-377bb0d64bc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-390" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is computed as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx\tf(0.0)\\Delta x_1+f(0.8)\\Delta x_2+f(1.6)\\Delta x_3,\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i+1}-x_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.8$ | $1.6$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Delta{x}$ | $0.8$ | $0.8$ | $0.4$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 0.8+5\\cdot 0.8+10\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.6+4+4 \\\\  & =9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "797ec111-8928-5875-8f94-9a423ff558f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7228" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[-4,2].$ If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $28,$ then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula for the left Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-4)}}+{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(5+3+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(8+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+2k & =28 \\\\ 2k & =12 \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "60eae905-fa87-5eac-bff2-8326ae41ca1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7230" :question/problem "Suppose a left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)=(2x-4)^2$ over the interval $[0,2].$ Is the resulting estimation an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/02/02b3449f0ccdb2f60eb489aec1b34485ee381b644a52eea7f5394e16851b6cff.png)\n\nBecause the function is *decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da9d2c8-f87f-52ab-bc1e-affb04efcc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$400$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534291} {:answer/id #uuid "0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534294} {:answer/id #uuid "3caddbf5-505a-5c83-800d-32cdc8dd40d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$800$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534381} {:answer/id #uuid "65cd7e27-fab6-576d-9c25-07914a986223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,000$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534471} {:answer/id #uuid "e9602873-3dbc-505a-b6a6-91eb730e5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\,000$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534294} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c6bcac-1b81-5ea4-b234-754b353a54a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115676" :question/problem "A tank is filling with soda. The table below gives the rate $R(t)$, in liters per minute, at which the tank is filling at certain moments since it started.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{liters/minute})$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ | $90$ |\n\nGiven that the tank is empty when $t=0,$ use a left Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,12]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of soda in the tank after $12$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=12,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{12-0}{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R({t}_{0})+R({t}_{1})+…+R({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(R(0)+R(3)+R(6)+R(9))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(30+40+60+70)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =200\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume in the tank. So, the volume is $600$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0960c82b-6c1b-5b1f-8bf9-41ec598d133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534278} {:answer/id #uuid "45fc19c2-908b-50ae-ba05-376e1cbaa786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534398} {:answer/id #uuid "52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534429} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8030cb-2dd5-50f4-a3eb-3a5316c29433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534499} {:answer/id #uuid "9681dbd2-1094-52d5-b2bd-83e405cca6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71bc7f8-343e-57a7-96e4-46b5c22b0949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" :question/math-academy-id "q-115688" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-3,3]$ with a regular step size. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $4$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(-3)+f(-1)+f(1))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be $4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c60a466-a39a-5a5b-bb84-5d4cb7876254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534355} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1b0e03-2b27-5442-9fcf-fd518b599444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f63f2ef-7e04-5845-a994-8b3a76a5610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534622} {:answer/id #uuid "a35d4692-5d9f-53e3-93e2-8277320977bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534635} {:answer/id #uuid "da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534755} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c68885-5ff0-588b-a710-c9a4d588c9db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" :question/math-academy-id "q-115695" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ad/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc50b29-7baf-5a27-96d5-142128ec93ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534624} {:answer/id #uuid "a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534626} {:answer/id #uuid "b23a1ed8-832f-51a4-9037-8703bcf7ddc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534665} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a87ab8-97db-50f2-b37e-b52d8b7bc07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534741} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d6386f-3f09-5d69-8338-27e6df95af4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534626} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b71fa70-bd24-5e39-8659-a1858750b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" :question/math-academy-id "q-19890" :question/problem "A speedboat travels downstream on a river. The table below gives the speed $v(t)$, in miles per hour, of the boat at certain moments during its journey.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{mi/hour})$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $v(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total distance traveled by the speedboat.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v({x}_{0})+v({t}_{1})+…+v({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(v(0)+v(0.5)+v(1)+v(1.5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(10+35+40+65)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled by the speedboat. So, the distance is $75\\,\\text{mi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534436} :answer-field/id #uuid "094a080c-b2c0-5da9-a43a-31f0cd0dae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315694" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[2,8]$ with a regular step size.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $18$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(2)+f(4)+f(6))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points are $2,4,6,8$ with three subintervals, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each left-endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈((-3)+5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A=18$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2k & =18 \\\\ 2k & =14 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534775} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4210f50-ff89-5293-88ab-8316c3879985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315696" :question/problem "Maya runs a lemonade stand. The table below gives the selling rate $r(t)$ (in liters per hour) over a $6$-hour period starting at noon.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{L/hour})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold over the $6$-hour period (with three subintervals defined by the given points) is $28$ liters, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(0)+r(2)+r(4))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by Maya. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+12 & =28 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310a5659-c828-5ea7-a022-5d72cdb1227d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "310a5659-c828-5ea7-a022-5d72cdb1227d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534361} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e144a86-436c-5e1b-b383-9ed170d66f18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "991c1ace-0aaf-57b9-84aa-e45b1d07c8fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-315714" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$ \n\n$A≈$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(2)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(6)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12+6+10 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609bcbfa-3548-57cf-bcc3-93d490bcb531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534460} {:answer/id #uuid "7fef99b6-9e65-5243-850a-68688028042a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534542} {:answer/id #uuid "df771a6e-1232-5f6a-84e1-694037e34463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534762} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fddebf-ef73-5795-a7f6-950173f0b125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df771a6e-1232-5f6a-84e1-694037e34463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534762} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd8deb-7d74-51bf-8a85-e4c7b6829053" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a769d530-b6a1-5708-87df-5205a70c420b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534639} {:answer/id #uuid "b24be7fe-23bc-5f34-826f-f1abc2a0549a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534666} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c006f8-9c2c-5582-91b2-eae40baaa5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exact value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a769d530-b6a1-5708-87df-5205a70c420b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534639} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0827ea4-90d5-5a4c-b262-f414a3213a8d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09afc441-68cd-5e99-bd07-fc18988c6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534280} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfdebaf-418b-55bc-ae86-c86ee148325a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09afc441-68cd-5e99-bd07-fc18988c6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534280} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d1e6f2-d3d9-54c9-8ea2-99f5774ef677" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e9c9afd-571f-54f0-b5de-d6a5e5729d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346697" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "![](images/e8/e8cd8966d69260c77685d349c97eceb1545686d347f6cdb827ad470880797bf5.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+3+4)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[0,1],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534476} {:answer/id #uuid "b971d682-7baf-5c86-8f72-52ba7b01ea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534476} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022b11-7e38-5845-8ce8-d823ff176f78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfeaaed-4260-554a-aed3-a5bacbddffde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534450} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5d3818-a0f9-5df3-97cf-a289e19e25f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534575} {:answer/id #uuid "e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534779} {:answer/id #uuid "eac4869b-ddab-5f08-9de9-9c32f40be8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534779} :answer-field/id #uuid "228f7032-0430-5206-88b5-0be9f2a8574f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15977689-22a7-5a1e-aa5e-b101b88ce8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exact value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534314} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe08a89-41ef-5c59-af03-7a5095196299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534625} {:answer/id #uuid "c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63a1ae7-518a-536a-919b-d5fff57d94d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" :question/math-academy-id "q-346698" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534306} :answer-field/id #uuid "60816e31-a460-50bd-9564-d4219818a430" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-364487" :question/problem "Estimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/27/275b62fbec1c1a550cb23b481ec54c1e12d3d42e70cf95cd917f1b932638add4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5))\\cdot 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-1$ at each left endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $15$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+8+15+24)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0775e6be-7272-5f9a-8537-613fe2aca146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534273} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee9e04a-1a87-5ade-ae29-aeb468fc39bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534418} {:answer/id #uuid "71be97d8-0a27-584a-a580-9b316ab3f234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534504} {:answer/id #uuid "94a14362-dee8-5090-bdfe-489ff119872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534592} {:answer/id #uuid "b3395e48-19f7-5981-8efa-e50a1b6ca1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94a14362-dee8-5090-bdfe-489ff119872f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{pounds}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534592} :answer-field/id #uuid "79db62af-3aac-5dd0-86a5-a5be6d93bc13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7303a5f4-0c27-5036-a2ee-f35a42950785" :question/math-academy-id "q-49103" :question/problem "Daniel decides to go on a diet. The table below gives the rate $w(t)$, in pounds per week, at which he loses weight over a period of $7$ weeks.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{week})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $w(t)\\,(\\text{pounds/week})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $w(t)$ over the interval $[0,7]$ with three subintervals to approximate Daniel's total weight loss." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈w(0)Δ{t}_{1}+w(1)Δ{t}_{2}+w(3)Δ{t}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-$ |\n| $w(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1+4+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total weight lost by Daniel. He lost approximately $9$ pounds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e29fb0-b035-5d75-87d7-cd58bd0e6de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b5cce5427550d19bb55a8dcf609e22a8a45be1f2c44285ec97c9b70278616171.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067427} {:answer/id #uuid "da3748ad-eb6b-5657-b64a-dde5ec7d568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f70e633c3881bf78a2652141979ba9f87b95744a896b9ce11fca9d6f3af9d716.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067428} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0ef57-6891-55fa-98c4-2c63fecf7247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/376b43aca96daddb245dcc5f14f655ee726848370dbf6a11db617c7e88aba597.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067429} {:answer/id #uuid "13849db4-23b6-5fcb-abf2-ba531c40c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c0654783dff2b22fcf834e37c8b6872a262871971438e0cdc7a6e3399684220.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067430} {:answer/id #uuid "7525a1f5-f389-5bbe-81be-be2f8b230f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7525a1f5-f389-5bbe-81be-be2f8b230f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067431} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4794cc-e7f3-589f-8563-a0062f45afe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "965cb62a-f834-5614-8e95-ef5ea2ae941c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49109" :question/problem "A left Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=3$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is decreasing over $x\\in [-1,3]$ is the following. \n\n![](images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f29be3e-c6e6-5765-9a36-e6c2d6d79201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534327} {:answer/id #uuid "3a55f2f0-027a-5d72-83af-690691e5a767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534375} {:answer/id #uuid "65beece7-eb0c-5403-82c1-f156ba1cf8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534470} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae403a-a02c-5946-910e-8a8432a1326f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "215\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534601} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4d68f7-a6fa-588c-a0d8-5ee96f4c9160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "221\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ae403a-a02c-5946-910e-8a8432a1326f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "215\\,\\text{km}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534601} :answer-field/id #uuid "567dbcb3-850d-5e82-b075-2d9721531795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "79154f2c-c86a-5c07-9c5a-51aae4f7090e" :question/math-academy-id "q-51130" :question/problem "The following table shows the speed $v$, in $\\text{km/min},$ at certain times during a family car trip over a period of $60$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{min})$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,(\\text{km/min})$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nAssuming that the family traveled $130\\,\\text{km}$ during the first hour of the trip, use the left Riemann sum to approximate the total distance traveled during the first two hours.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈v(60)Δ{t}_{1}+v(70)Δ{t}_{2}+v(80)Δ{t}_{3}+v(95)Δ{t}_{4}+v(110)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $10$ | $10$ | $15$ | $15$ | $10$ | $-$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 1.5+10\\cdot 2+15\\cdot 1+15\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15+20+15+15+20 \\\\  & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the distance covered in $60$ minutes of travel, from $t=60$ minutes to $t=120$ minutes. The distance traveled in the first two hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=130+85=215\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad12563-d23b-51db-b300-732306b79dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534285} {:answer/id #uuid "0f21d02b-4305-55e4-bc13-651211646064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534298} {:answer/id #uuid "578076e5-1b98-55d4-86f0-4cb2184637ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534442} {:answer/id #uuid "654da08d-c6bd-525b-97c6-8ab6ccebf7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534468} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2fb41f-dd92-59eb-9e69-41eb33f43ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "578076e5-1b98-55d4-86f0-4cb2184637ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534442} :answer-field/id #uuid "55543bee-eec5-5011-a8cf-a59bb2260121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9dcd5b8-f37b-55de-ab7a-6d8ec569a890" :question/math-academy-id "q-71866" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(3)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(7)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3+8+10+27 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210cb1e7-4d4f-521d-94ea-b92ddb31ec30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534333} {:answer/id #uuid "729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534507} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f94a57-31fa-5cc6-ae1d-146b8abaa792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534630} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dec5bb-a6e8-5021-8f8f-827f57c20855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534778} {:answer/id #uuid "efdc7fbe-3706-5051-a3b0-a9413406ae28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534507} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9732c7-7fe4-5d54-92ce-d54efeda2989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" :question/math-academy-id "q-74490" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0.5,2.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e3f499cbc36c9dca7e759b67a8fd9e94959039f3403832e1d60b9ed598c44dd.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $2.25$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1.25+2+2.25+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515320ab-5f99-5b9e-8e87-3bb603a6ba82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534426} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534455} {:answer/id #uuid "83da73b0-48f3-586d-85b3-eb18d7a17400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.57,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534550} {:answer/id #uuid "da4e4c5a-7f58-5eea-ab10-1c79a44758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.73,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534756} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fb0583-ff57-5c40-ac23-4c564c2513d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.73,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534455} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c4ed498-d752-509d-b505-ce28b302f4d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" :question/math-academy-id "q-74503" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x+2$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/2c/2cd664908ea218fecf5a33ea3312bd883f5495d64fc0da8387632a7d2e17c26a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+1.25+2+3.25)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,3],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6cc51c-f75d-5de1-9ca4-6c0b80fc3b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534290} {:answer/id #uuid "3efdacea-8c05-5949-8ede-c74ab5d29823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534384} {:answer/id #uuid "99995787-ed19-5679-a5bf-00ac8c2df729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534608} {:answer/id #uuid "b3228cb4-6fcd-521e-bd34-d956adb0b6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534667} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b0dbbd-4055-5289-993a-43db6e8d830e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b0dbbd-4055-5289-993a-43db6e8d830e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534673} :answer-field/id #uuid "281bd471-7bf7-50a8-983f-523469e95e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dc2cb61-7e14-5e92-9670-4b835f928971" :question/math-academy-id "q-74506" :question/problem "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[-3,5].$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[-3,5].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(-3)Δ{x}_{1}+f(-1)Δ{x}_{2}+f(0)Δ{x}_{3}+f(3)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈-1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2+1+6+8 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185282f2-7ff6-5c6b-b9b9-e4a2049ff490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534320} {:answer/id #uuid "1a540d1d-50f6-5433-a351-e20154df9816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534322} {:answer/id #uuid "2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534337} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2d11e1-346f-5678-87b0-f47869751fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534411} {:answer/id #uuid "9abf62e4-c1ec-5172-86a6-3a5ad254967f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534337} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5a1c82-2697-5ad0-92be-ffc88f085f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" :question/math-academy-id "q-86288" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,10]$ with a regular step size. Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,10]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(4)+f(7))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the interval $[1,10]$ is divided into $3$ equal subintervals, the width of each subinterval is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the left endpoints:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+0)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a2b37e-f7fd-5fb0-af6d-9e771b691d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534269} {:answer/id #uuid "5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534448} {:answer/id #uuid "732c7367-4e22-5a0a-8514-ecf1b997bfbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534510} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3ad3-6f87-5264-ad1c-f19f7f542b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534518} {:answer/id #uuid "959c80c5-314d-5889-8b74-145a524b94ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534448} :answer-field/id #uuid "63889206-4140-5315-b206-7bddd19df74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" :question/math-academy-id "q-86296" :question/problem "John is selling juice at a market stall. The table below gives the rate $r(t)$ of juice sold over a period of $6$ hours, from $1\\,\\text{p.m.}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{liters/hour})$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters John sold during the given period has a value of $16$ liters, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(1)+r(3)+r(5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+5) \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by John. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+10 & =16 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228eec43-938a-5953-916c-999c2cbbaa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534339} {:answer/id #uuid "6776dc08-9fdf-51fd-996a-bfab15c3331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534474} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534564} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78173e-a4fa-5da6-a57c-b9e952b87134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534616} {:answer/id #uuid "a159d0cd-4dba-5b36-b32e-909f05583e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534564} :answer-field/id #uuid "e817f9ef-3f03-51be-bb7b-0bc236498520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86298" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate of change of a city's temperature over a $10$-hour period, starting at $2\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{h})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T\\,{(}^{∘}\\text{C}/\\text{h})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}$ has a value of $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=T(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{T({t}_{0})+T({t}_{1})+…+T({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(T(2)+T(4)+T(6)+T(8)+T(10))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $T(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+10) \\\\  & =2k+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature is $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+20 & =26 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
